WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.205 --> 00:00:01.971
- Yes, I believe in the Internet.

00:00:01.971 --> 00:00:04.768
I believe it'll get increasingly popular,

00:00:04.768 --> 00:00:06.545
and we're doing some neat, new things

00:00:06.545 --> 00:00:07.665
to take advantage of that.

00:00:07.665 --> 00:00:11.198
When you have the level
of success that we've had,

00:00:11.198 --> 00:00:13.248
when you have a business
that's important as this

00:00:13.248 --> 00:00:15.204
with this many competitors,

00:00:15.204 --> 00:00:17.286
you're going to have people
saying some nasty things.

00:00:17.286 --> 00:00:19.440
The lives all over the world should be

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.149
treated with the same
value that we treat lives

00:00:22.149 --> 00:00:23.573
here in the rich world.

00:00:23.573 --> 00:00:26.532
If you ask people across
the United States,

00:00:26.532 --> 00:00:30.219
"Is the future going to
be better than the past?",

00:00:30.219 --> 00:00:31.973
most say no.

00:00:31.973 --> 00:00:33.589
- He's an American entrepreneur,

00:00:33.589 --> 00:00:36.293
author, investor, and philanthropist.

00:00:36.293 --> 00:00:40.338
In 1975, he co-founded Microsoft
which went on to become

00:00:40.338 --> 00:00:43.380
the world's largest PC software company.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.557
He's currently the richest
person in the world

00:00:45.557 --> 00:00:48.976
with an estimated net worth
of $84 billion dollars.

00:00:48.976 --> 00:00:50.944
He's Bill Gates, and here's my take on

00:00:50.944 --> 00:00:53.864
his top 10 rules for success, volume two.

00:00:53.864 --> 00:00:55.573
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:55.573 --> 00:00:56.983
and stick around all the way to the end

00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:58.370
for a special bonus clip.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.769
As always, as Bill is talking,

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:02.096
if he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:01:02.096 --> 00:01:03.427
please leave it in the comments below

00:01:03.427 --> 00:01:04.260
and put quotes around it

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.893
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:06.893 --> 00:01:07.726
Enjoy.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.897
(inspirational music)

00:01:21.503 --> 00:01:22.553
- You really have to believe

00:01:22.553 --> 00:01:24.558
the Internet's going to be mainstream,

00:01:24.558 --> 00:01:27.223
a lot of people are going
to get out there and use it,

00:01:27.223 --> 00:01:29.584
and that they're going to be willing

00:01:29.584 --> 00:01:30.919
to pay for some content.

00:01:30.919 --> 00:01:34.609
- [Charlie] Is that the
operating idea that you have?

00:01:34.609 --> 00:01:36.470
- Each of these businesses

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.904
is an entrepreneurial business.

00:01:38.904 --> 00:01:40.860
The overreaching theme is that yes,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.260
I believe in the Internet.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.092
I believe it'll get increasingly popular,

00:01:45.092 --> 00:01:46.842
and we're doing some neat, new things

00:01:46.842 --> 00:01:48.777
to take advantage of that.

00:01:48.777 --> 00:01:50.646
- You've got a lot of cash on hand.

00:01:50.646 --> 00:01:51.511
Yes?

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:52.344
- Right.

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:53.758
- [Charlie] All right (laughing).

00:01:53.758 --> 00:01:55.277
It puts you in an enviable position.

00:01:55.277 --> 00:01:58.135
You can experiment with a
lot of entrepreneurial ideas

00:01:58.135 --> 00:02:01.027
and see what sticks and what flies.

00:02:01.027 --> 00:02:03.950
- We're in business to make money.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.011
- [Charlie] The other thing is providing

00:02:05.011 --> 00:02:07.188
such a cash flow for you.

00:02:07.188 --> 00:02:09.114
- [Bill] Well, it all
belongs to the shareholders.

00:02:09.114 --> 00:02:09.947
- [Charlie] Yes.

00:02:09.947 --> 00:02:10.780
- We're not dilettantes.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.613
- [Charlie] No, I know.

00:02:11.613 --> 00:02:13.904
- We are business people, and

00:02:13.904 --> 00:02:16.871
it is true that if you find an idea

00:02:16.871 --> 00:02:19.314
that requires three or four years

00:02:19.314 --> 00:02:23.088
of improvement and patience
and really sticking with it

00:02:23.088 --> 00:02:24.630
that we're very good at that.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.992
Take Windows, which we bet our company on.

00:02:26.992 --> 00:02:29.550
Everybody who doubted that would succeed,

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.029
IBM did not support us in that.

00:02:32.029 --> 00:02:34.355
It took longer than we expected,

00:02:34.355 --> 00:02:36.433
over four years before finally

00:02:36.433 --> 00:02:38.436
graphical interface got popular

00:02:38.436 --> 00:02:39.787
and now people take it for granted.

00:02:39.787 --> 00:02:41.395
It's part of every personal computer,

00:02:41.395 --> 00:02:44.107
and you just expect it to be there.

00:02:44.107 --> 00:02:46.961
That was one of the grand
successes of the company.

00:02:46.961 --> 00:02:49.053
In the same way we're
betting on the Internet,

00:02:49.053 --> 00:02:51.408
that our tools there will be popular

00:02:51.408 --> 00:02:53.488
and that a few of these content plays

00:02:53.488 --> 00:02:56.023
that we've decided to get involved in,

00:02:56.023 --> 00:02:58.493
that the scale and the users

00:02:58.493 --> 00:03:01.051
will make those into great businesses.

00:03:01.051 --> 00:03:03.528
Well, certainly every product we do

00:03:03.528 --> 00:03:07.445
is absolutely as capable
as it can possibly be.

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:11.487
There's no holding back.

00:03:11.487 --> 00:03:13.443
The people at Microsoft

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:15.311
come into work everyday

00:03:15.311 --> 00:03:17.198
building the best products they can,

00:03:17.198 --> 00:03:19.086
and they're very proud.

00:03:19.086 --> 00:03:21.416
Go into schools and see how
kids are using this stuff.

00:03:21.416 --> 00:03:24.133
Go and ask people about
how their jobs have changed

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:26.137
because of the personal computer.

00:03:26.137 --> 00:03:26.970
- [Interviewer] Sure.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.735
- We're sitting there
listening to our customers

00:03:28.735 --> 00:03:32.168
saying how they'd like
to make things better.

00:03:32.168 --> 00:03:33.879
We do absolutely our best job.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:36.314
In fact, that's why we've been successful.

00:03:36.314 --> 00:03:39.105
Anybody who holds back in this business

00:03:39.105 --> 00:03:41.724
isn't going to be around for long because

00:03:41.724 --> 00:03:44.551
this is a business where
you always have to be

00:03:44.551 --> 00:03:47.349
moving at a very rapid pace.

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:50.054
- You have, for years, ever
since you were a little boy,

00:03:50.054 --> 00:03:52.438
I hear, have been called a nerd.

00:03:52.438 --> 00:03:53.271
Do you care?

00:03:53.271 --> 00:03:54.480
Does it hurt you?

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.067
- Well, nerd means a lot of things.

00:03:56.067 --> 00:03:57.645
I'm somebody who can

00:03:57.645 --> 00:04:00.826
sit and read a book about
science for hours on end.

00:04:00.826 --> 00:04:03.010
I don't mind being labeled as somebody

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.425
who finds that interesting.

00:04:05.425 --> 00:04:07.151
- [Barbara] There is hardly a day

00:04:07.151 --> 00:04:09.755
in which something is not
written about Microsoft.

00:04:09.755 --> 00:04:11.607
It has become almost a frenzy.

00:04:11.607 --> 00:04:15.195
You are being called the evil empire.

00:04:15.195 --> 00:04:16.526
Forget being called a nerd.

00:04:16.526 --> 00:04:20.693
You are now being called
arrogant, greedy, the Devil.

00:04:22.192 --> 00:04:23.097
You've read all this stuff

00:04:23.097 --> 00:04:24.769
or heard that this has been said.

00:04:24.769 --> 00:04:27.149
What do you say about all of this?

00:04:27.149 --> 00:04:29.418
- Well, it's a very competitive business.

00:04:29.418 --> 00:04:30.457
- That's all?

00:04:30.457 --> 00:04:31.487
- [Bill] Oh no, absolutely.

00:04:31.487 --> 00:04:34.991
When you have the level
of success that we've had,

00:04:34.991 --> 00:04:36.984
when you have a business
that's important as this

00:04:36.984 --> 00:04:38.819
with this many competitors,

00:04:38.819 --> 00:04:41.206
you're going to have people
saying some nasty things.

00:04:41.206 --> 00:04:42.797
You have to learn a little bit

00:04:42.797 --> 00:04:44.666
not to take it too personally.

00:04:44.666 --> 00:04:46.786
- You went to Harvard and you dropped out.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:49.040
Have you ever thought how
your life could be better off

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.504
if you had gotten your Harvard degree?

00:04:51.504 --> 00:04:53.557
- [Bill] Well, I'm a weird dropout

00:04:53.557 --> 00:04:56.125
because I take college
courses all the time.

00:04:56.125 --> 00:05:00.299
I love learning company
courses and things,

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:03.230
so I love being a student.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.552
There were smart people around.

00:05:05.552 --> 00:05:06.950
They fed you.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:11.288
They gave you these nice grades
that made you feel smart.

00:05:11.288 --> 00:05:13.371
I feel it was unfortunate

00:05:14.336 --> 00:05:15.881
that I didn't get to stay there

00:05:15.881 --> 00:05:19.208
but I don't think I missed
any knowledge because

00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:20.882
whatever I needed to learn

00:05:20.882 --> 00:05:23.326
I was still in a learning mode.

00:05:23.326 --> 00:05:26.059
People who have been successful are often,

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:27.681
not always, pretty fanatical about

00:05:27.681 --> 00:05:29.557
the thing they're trying to do.

00:05:29.557 --> 00:05:31.974
I remember one industry panel

00:05:32.918 --> 00:05:35.989
where there were about seven
people and the debate was

00:05:35.989 --> 00:05:38.356
would the computer interface
be this character-mode thing

00:05:38.356 --> 00:05:42.518
or would be it be graphic user interface?

00:05:42.518 --> 00:05:46.432
At the time, the graphic user
interface stuff was so slow

00:05:46.432 --> 00:05:47.365
it was laughable.

00:05:47.365 --> 00:05:49.341
Writing software for it was so bad.

00:05:49.341 --> 00:05:50.924
It was Windows 1.0.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.225
The people on the panel
were saying, "No, no.

00:05:55.225 --> 00:05:57.703
"This is kind of a stupid thing."

00:05:57.703 --> 00:05:59.153
I would say, "No, believe me.

00:05:59.153 --> 00:06:00.710
"This'll be great."

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:03.543
One of the guys on the panel said,

00:06:04.448 --> 00:06:06.107
"Hey, Bill is wrong,

00:06:06.107 --> 00:06:08.447
"but Bill works harder
than the rest of us.

00:06:08.447 --> 00:06:09.917
"Even though it's the wrong solution,

00:06:09.917 --> 00:06:11.479
"he's likely to succeed."

00:06:11.479 --> 00:06:14.236
That was the best compliment I ever had.

00:06:14.236 --> 00:06:15.774
Just by working day and night,

00:06:15.774 --> 00:06:19.441
I could send the industry
in some direction.

00:06:20.906 --> 00:06:23.746
I was fanatical in that
period of time, that is,

00:06:23.746 --> 00:06:26.677
I didn't believe in vacation.

00:06:26.677 --> 00:06:29.094
I didn't believe in weekends.

00:06:31.081 --> 00:06:32.903
It turned out that worked for me that

00:06:32.903 --> 00:06:35.571
we got our company going at a speed

00:06:35.571 --> 00:06:38.096
that allowed it to make mistakes faster

00:06:38.096 --> 00:06:41.084
than other people were and
kind of see those mistakes.

00:06:41.084 --> 00:06:43.223
- [Charlie] Do you worry
about Linux as a competition

00:06:43.223 --> 00:06:44.816
for operating systems?

00:06:44.816 --> 00:06:45.899
- [Bill] Yes.

00:06:48.717 --> 00:06:50.586
We've competed against various forms

00:06:50.586 --> 00:06:51.693
of Unix over the years,

00:06:51.693 --> 00:06:54.678
and Linux is growing in popularity

00:06:54.678 --> 00:06:57.011
and definitely a competitor.

00:06:58.157 --> 00:07:01.196
- How threatening a competitor?

00:07:01.196 --> 00:07:03.721
- It's a competitor we
take very seriously.

00:07:03.721 --> 00:07:07.475
I think, you know, part
of Microsoft's success

00:07:07.475 --> 00:07:10.709
is that we don't underestimate

00:07:10.709 --> 00:07:13.898
the importance of the work
that other companies are doing.

00:07:13.898 --> 00:07:15.479
We look out.

00:07:15.479 --> 00:07:19.728
We think, "Are there
aspects of that that are

00:07:19.728 --> 00:07:20.968
"best practices?"

00:07:20.968 --> 00:07:23.662
For example, online supports

00:07:23.662 --> 00:07:26.084
and the community things that are done

00:07:26.084 --> 00:07:28.751
around Linux are done very well.

00:07:31.849 --> 00:07:35.042
We're thinking about it and
making sure that our innovation

00:07:35.042 --> 00:07:37.445
will make sure that our
value and our leadership

00:07:37.445 --> 00:07:38.688
stays in front.

00:07:38.688 --> 00:07:39.929
First I met Warren.

00:07:39.929 --> 00:07:42.380
We were talking about
getting together and doing

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:43.213
something again.

00:07:43.213 --> 00:07:44.877
He pulled out his calendar,

00:07:44.877 --> 00:07:47.210
and the pages were so blank.

00:07:48.117 --> 00:07:49.879
I said, "Wow,

00:07:49.879 --> 00:07:52.352
"you've managed to avoid getting tied in

00:07:52.352 --> 00:07:55.751
"to a lot of kind of
meaningless activity."

00:07:55.751 --> 00:07:58.833
Warren said, "Yeah, you
have to be good at saying no

00:07:58.833 --> 00:08:01.985
"and picking the things that
really make a difference."

00:08:01.985 --> 00:08:03.725
That's one of many things
I've learned from Warren,

00:08:03.725 --> 00:08:05.834
but that's one of my favorites

00:08:05.834 --> 00:08:09.288
so I can blame it on him
whenever I'm turning things down.

00:08:09.288 --> 00:08:10.605
- You have made

00:08:10.605 --> 00:08:13.443
a significant contribution

00:08:13.443 --> 00:08:15.534
in the fight against AIDS in Africa.

00:08:15.534 --> 00:08:18.451
You have funded, to a large degree,

00:08:19.329 --> 00:08:21.829
vaccines and vaccine research.

00:08:23.022 --> 00:08:24.867
Give me a sense of where that's directed

00:08:24.867 --> 00:08:27.242
and what success you think

00:08:27.242 --> 00:08:30.202
we're making and your
own particular interest

00:08:30.202 --> 00:08:32.902
in public health in the world.

00:08:32.902 --> 00:08:34.158
- Well, this is a real passion for me.

00:08:34.158 --> 00:08:35.836
- [Charlie] I know.

00:08:35.836 --> 00:08:38.336
- I'll give a succinct answer.

00:08:40.397 --> 00:08:43.909
People, I think, don't
have a full awareness

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:48.259
how four billion of the six
billion people on this planet

00:08:48.259 --> 00:08:51.092
don't have basic health needs met.

00:08:51.945 --> 00:08:54.295
The death rate of infants,

00:08:54.295 --> 00:08:57.255
the epidemic infectious diseases

00:08:57.255 --> 00:09:01.173
that are just a way of
life in most of the world

00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:04.563
don't exist in the developed world,

00:09:04.563 --> 00:09:07.634
yet the research isn't going on

00:09:07.634 --> 00:09:11.030
for the medicines that would
get rid of these diseases.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:12.960
Even diseases like tuberculosis,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:16.134
where for a few hundred
dollars lives can be saved,

00:09:16.134 --> 00:09:17.813
that's not being done.

00:09:17.813 --> 00:09:19.386
Vaccines aren't being moved

00:09:19.386 --> 00:09:21.470
from the rich world to the poor world.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.627
AIDS is the most extreme where

00:09:24.627 --> 00:09:26.892
the prevention measures
that could prevent this

00:09:26.892 --> 00:09:29.324
from becoming a huge epidemic

00:09:29.324 --> 00:09:31.849
in countries like India and Nigeria

00:09:31.849 --> 00:09:34.697
and many countries where it's not

00:09:34.697 --> 00:09:36.298
in huge percentages today.

00:09:36.298 --> 00:09:38.394
- [Charlie] It hasn't reached
the proportions of Africa.

00:09:38.394 --> 00:09:40.233
- [Bill] The right things, I think,

00:09:40.233 --> 00:09:42.400
are not being prioritized.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.261
The foundation that my wife and I have

00:09:45.261 --> 00:09:48.158
have taken as its top priority

00:09:48.158 --> 00:09:49.911
these world health issues,

00:09:49.911 --> 00:09:53.161
the research, getting the vaccines out.

00:09:54.392 --> 00:09:56.203
It's been fascinating to learn about this

00:09:56.203 --> 00:09:59.152
and to try to say that all these lives,

00:09:59.152 --> 00:10:00.447
the lives all over the world,

00:10:00.447 --> 00:10:02.808
should be treated with the same value

00:10:02.808 --> 00:10:06.582
that we treat lives
here in the rich world.

00:10:06.582 --> 00:10:08.776
- [Charlie] Just to sum up in a sense,

00:10:08.776 --> 00:10:10.761
what you have learned

00:10:10.761 --> 00:10:13.013
from your own involvement,

00:10:13.013 --> 00:10:15.537
from funding it in terms of

00:10:15.537 --> 00:10:17.672
more money than had ever been applied,

00:10:17.672 --> 00:10:19.370
is that it can make a difference.

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:20.806
- Absolutely.

00:10:20.806 --> 00:10:22.223
Millions of lives

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:27.153
can be and should be saved
through these efforts.

00:10:28.039 --> 00:10:29.595
We can make a difference,

00:10:29.595 --> 00:10:32.125
and we can encourage
others to get involved.

00:10:32.125 --> 00:10:33.013
- [Charlie] That was my last question.

00:10:33.013 --> 00:10:34.150
Is that happening?

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:36.804
As you fund these efforts,

00:10:36.804 --> 00:10:39.966
is it becoming a kind of
wedge that other governments

00:10:39.966 --> 00:10:42.974
and other foundations and other sources

00:10:42.974 --> 00:10:44.778
are following suit or matching?

00:10:44.778 --> 00:10:48.296
- We've seen a good start, I would say.

00:10:48.296 --> 00:10:51.046
The vaccine fund we put together,

00:10:52.104 --> 00:10:54.823
we put in $750 million now.

00:10:54.823 --> 00:10:58.606
Governments have agreed
to put up $500 million

00:10:58.606 --> 00:11:00.437
in addition to that.

00:11:00.437 --> 00:11:02.571
In the AIDS area,

00:11:02.571 --> 00:11:03.809
some of the things we've backed

00:11:03.809 --> 00:11:06.253
have been able to
attract additional money.

00:11:06.253 --> 00:11:08.233
Having said that,

00:11:08.233 --> 00:11:10.851
this is a cause that somehow

00:11:10.851 --> 00:11:12.453
deserves more visibility

00:11:12.453 --> 00:11:14.471
because when you talk to people about this

00:11:14.471 --> 00:11:17.776
and they hear about malaria or AIDS,

00:11:17.776 --> 00:11:18.612
they really do care

00:11:18.612 --> 00:11:22.380
and they think their
government's doing more.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:23.313
When I'm not working,

00:11:23.313 --> 00:11:27.208
a lot of my creativity is
going into what could be done

00:11:27.208 --> 00:11:30.964
to show people that their
money would be well-spent,

00:11:30.964 --> 00:11:32.659
it would make a difference,

00:11:32.659 --> 00:11:34.395
and try and make sure

00:11:34.395 --> 00:11:37.353
that world health gets more priority.

00:11:37.353 --> 00:11:39.221
- [Charlie] Then you can save lives.

00:11:39.221 --> 00:11:40.427
- That's what it's all about.

00:11:40.427 --> 00:11:43.273
If you ask people across
the Unites States,

00:11:43.273 --> 00:11:46.981
"Is the future going to
be better than the past?",

00:11:46.981 --> 00:11:48.931
most say, "No.

00:11:48.931 --> 00:11:52.688
"My kids will be worse off than I am."

00:11:52.688 --> 00:11:55.068
They think innovation won't make the world

00:11:55.068 --> 00:11:58.186
better for them or their children.

00:11:58.186 --> 00:11:59.186
Who's right,

00:12:00.066 --> 00:12:03.863
the people who say innovation
will create new possibilities

00:12:03.863 --> 00:12:06.522
and make the world better

00:12:06.522 --> 00:12:09.807
or the people who see a
trend toward inequality

00:12:09.807 --> 00:12:12.153
and a decline in opportunity

00:12:12.153 --> 00:12:15.590
and don't think innovation
will change that?

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:19.358
The pessimists are wrong, in my view,

00:12:19.358 --> 00:12:21.284
but they're not crazy.

00:12:21.284 --> 00:12:24.483
If innovation is purely market-driven

00:12:24.483 --> 00:12:26.817
and we don't focus on the big inequities,

00:12:26.817 --> 00:12:30.941
then we could have amazing
advances and inventions

00:12:30.941 --> 00:12:34.368
that leave the world even more divided.

00:12:34.368 --> 00:12:37.022
We won't improve public schools.

00:12:37.022 --> 00:12:38.942
We won't cure malaria.

00:12:38.942 --> 00:12:40.692
We won't end poverty.

00:12:41.585 --> 00:12:45.387
We won't develop the
innovations poor farmers need

00:12:45.387 --> 00:12:49.120
to grow food in a changing climate.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:52.649
If our optimism doesn't
address the problems

00:12:52.649 --> 00:12:56.092
that affect so many of
our fellow human beings,

00:12:56.092 --> 00:12:59.551
then our optimism needs more empathy.

00:12:59.551 --> 00:13:02.584
If empathy channels our optimism,

00:13:02.584 --> 00:13:04.564
we will see the poverty

00:13:04.564 --> 00:13:07.130
and the disease and the poor schools.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.846
We will answer with our innovations,

00:13:09.846 --> 00:13:12.846
and we will surprise the pessimists.

00:13:14.379 --> 00:13:16.195
Over the next generation,

00:13:16.195 --> 00:13:17.526
you Stanford graduates

00:13:17.526 --> 00:13:20.725
will lead a new wave of innovation.

00:13:20.725 --> 00:13:23.883
Which problems will you decide to solve?

00:13:23.883 --> 00:13:25.680
If your world is wide,

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:28.107
you can create the future we all want.

00:13:28.107 --> 00:13:30.129
If your world is narrow,

00:13:30.129 --> 00:13:34.204
you may create the future
the pessimists fear.

00:13:34.204 --> 00:13:36.123
I started learning in Soweto

00:13:36.123 --> 00:13:39.770
that if we're going to make
our optimism matter to everyone

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:41.518
and empower people everywhere,

00:13:41.518 --> 00:13:45.174
we have to see the lives
of those most in need.

00:13:45.174 --> 00:13:48.066
If we have optimism without empathy,

00:13:48.066 --> 00:13:49.580
then it doesn't matter how much

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.973
we master the secrets of science.

00:13:51.973 --> 00:13:53.660
We're not really solving problems.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:56.664
We're just working on puzzles.

00:13:56.664 --> 00:14:00.199
I think most of you
have a broader worldview

00:14:00.199 --> 00:14:02.538
than I had at your age.

00:14:02.538 --> 00:14:05.792
You can do better at this than I did.

00:14:05.792 --> 00:14:07.959
If you put your hearts and minds to it,

00:14:07.959 --> 00:14:10.915
you can surprise the pessimists.

00:14:10.915 --> 00:14:12.951
We're eager to see it.

00:14:12.951 --> 00:14:15.430
- You're obviously going
to go down in history,

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:17.593
history books already set kind of thing,

00:14:17.593 --> 00:14:18.680
but what's the greatest misunderstanding

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:22.554
in your relationship and about each other?

00:14:22.554 --> 00:14:25.294
What would you say would be

00:14:25.294 --> 00:14:27.670
this idea of cat fight,

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:29.170
which of the many?

00:14:30.472 --> 00:14:32.918
- We've kept our marriage
secret for over a decade now.

00:14:32.918 --> 00:14:35.918
(audience laughing)

00:14:36.935 --> 00:14:37.846
- [Kara] Canada.

00:14:37.846 --> 00:14:39.513
That trip to Canada.

00:14:42.647 --> 00:14:45.647
(audience laughing)

00:14:48.975 --> 00:14:50.784
- I don't think either of us

00:14:50.784 --> 00:14:54.604
have anything to complain about in general

00:14:54.604 --> 00:14:57.153
and I know that the projects,

00:14:57.153 --> 00:15:00.624
like the Mac project, was
just an incredible thing,

00:15:00.624 --> 00:15:03.957
a fun thing where we were taking a risk.

00:15:06.166 --> 00:15:08.065
We did look a lot younger in that video.

00:15:08.065 --> 00:15:09.613
- We did.
(audience laughing)

00:15:09.613 --> 00:15:11.914
- [Kara] You looked 12 in the first one.

00:15:11.914 --> 00:15:12.747
(audience laughing)

00:15:12.747 --> 00:15:13.796
- That's how I try and look.

00:15:13.796 --> 00:15:14.629
- [Steve] He was 12.

00:15:14.629 --> 00:15:17.954
(audience laughing)

00:15:17.954 --> 00:15:20.589
- No, it's been fun to work together.

00:15:20.589 --> 00:15:22.047
I actually kind of miss some of the people

00:15:22.047 --> 00:15:23.670
who aren't around anymore.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:25.297
People come and go in this industry.

00:15:25.297 --> 00:15:28.699
It's nice when somebody sticks around.

00:15:28.699 --> 00:15:30.423
They have some context of all the things

00:15:30.423 --> 00:15:32.588
that have worked and not worked.

00:15:32.588 --> 00:15:33.957
The industry gets

00:15:33.957 --> 00:15:36.540
all crazy about some new thing.

00:15:39.111 --> 00:15:40.516
There's always this paradigm of

00:15:40.516 --> 00:15:42.385
the company that's successful
is going to go away

00:15:42.385 --> 00:15:43.218
and stuff like that.

00:15:43.218 --> 00:15:44.562
It's nice to have people

00:15:44.562 --> 00:15:47.926
seeing the waves and waves of that and yet

00:15:47.926 --> 00:15:49.461
been willing, when it counted,

00:15:49.461 --> 00:15:52.346
to take the risk to
bring in something new.

00:15:52.346 --> 00:15:53.995
- [Walt] Has it been important?

00:15:53.995 --> 00:15:56.421
One last question and
then we'll go to the--

00:15:56.421 --> 00:15:57.254
- I've got an answer for that too.

00:15:57.254 --> 00:15:58.209
- No, he didn't answer us
because he just said this--

00:15:58.209 --> 00:15:59.334
- I'm sorry, what?
- I have an answer

00:15:59.334 --> 00:16:00.167
for that too.
- Oh, I'm sorry.

00:16:00.167 --> 00:16:01.000
- He only talked about
his secret gay marriage.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:01.833
- Okay, yeah.

00:16:01.833 --> 00:16:02.666
I thought that was your answer.

00:16:02.666 --> 00:16:04.999
- No, that wasn't my answer.

00:16:05.937 --> 00:16:08.458
When Bill and I first met each other

00:16:08.458 --> 00:16:12.225
and worked together in the early days,

00:16:12.225 --> 00:16:16.625
generally we were both the
youngest guys in the room,

00:16:16.625 --> 00:16:18.077
right, individually or together.

00:16:18.077 --> 00:16:20.048
I'm about six months older than he is but

00:16:20.048 --> 00:16:21.719
roughly the same age.

00:16:21.719 --> 00:16:23.831
Now, when we're working at
our respective companies,

00:16:23.831 --> 00:16:24.911
I don't know about you, but

00:16:24.911 --> 00:16:28.168
I'm the oldest guy in the
room most of the time.

00:16:28.168 --> 00:16:30.268
That's why I love being here.

00:16:30.268 --> 00:16:34.435
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:16:35.916 --> 00:16:39.416
- [Walt] Happy to oblige, happy to oblige.

00:16:40.635 --> 00:16:42.402
- I think of most things in life

00:16:42.402 --> 00:16:44.420
as either a Bob Dylan or Beatles song,

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:48.089
but there's that one line
in that one Beatles song,

00:16:48.089 --> 00:16:49.297
"You and I have memories

00:16:49.297 --> 00:16:51.505
"longer than the road
that stretches out ahead",

00:16:51.505 --> 00:16:53.922
and that's clearly true here.

00:16:54.929 --> 00:16:55.762
- [Kara] It's sweet.

00:16:55.762 --> 00:16:56.595
- [Walt] You know what?

00:16:56.595 --> 00:16:57.558
I think we should end it there.

00:16:57.558 --> 00:16:58.430
Let's just end it there.

00:16:58.430 --> 00:16:59.631
- I might have a little tear right here.

00:16:59.631 --> 00:17:01.633
(audience laughing)

00:17:01.633 --> 00:17:02.466
- [Walt] Thank you.

00:17:02.466 --> 00:17:03.299
Thank you very much.

00:17:03.299 --> 00:17:04.132
- [Kara] Thank you so much.

00:17:04.132 --> 00:17:04.965
- Sure.

00:17:04.965 --> 00:17:08.132
(audience applauding)

00:17:10.896 --> 00:17:12.324
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:17:12.324 --> 00:17:14.008
This was an experimental video

00:17:14.008 --> 00:17:16.575
doing a volume two of a top 10 rules

00:17:16.575 --> 00:17:17.825
so I'm curious to figure out,

00:17:17.825 --> 00:17:18.939
did you guys like this?

00:17:18.939 --> 00:17:22.074
Do you want me to do more like
this in the future or not?

00:17:22.074 --> 00:17:23.065
Leave it in the comments below.

00:17:23.065 --> 00:17:23.898
Let me know.

00:17:23.898 --> 00:17:24.826
I also am curious to figure out

00:17:24.826 --> 00:17:26.508
which of the rules did you like the best.

00:17:26.508 --> 00:17:28.506
What is most applicable to your life

00:17:28.506 --> 00:17:29.898
or to your business right now?

00:17:29.898 --> 00:17:32.024
What immediate change
are you going to make

00:17:32.024 --> 00:17:33.515
after watching this video?

00:17:33.515 --> 00:17:34.628
Leave it in the comments,

00:17:34.628 --> 00:17:36.279
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:17:36.279 --> 00:17:38.514
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout-out to Tuan Nguyen.

00:17:38.514 --> 00:17:40.388
Thank you so much for picking up 10 copies

00:17:40.388 --> 00:17:42.274
of my book Your One Word

00:17:42.274 --> 00:17:44.608
and sharing it with some of
your friends and clients.

00:17:44.608 --> 00:17:47.208
I really, really, really,
really appreciate it.

00:17:47.208 --> 00:17:48.747
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:17:48.747 --> 00:17:50.036
I believe in you.

00:17:50.036 --> 00:17:52.398
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:17:52.398 --> 00:17:54.534
and whatever your One Word is.

00:17:54.534 --> 00:17:55.978
Much love.

00:17:55.978 --> 00:17:57.478
I'll see you soon.

00:18:03.023 --> 00:18:03.856
- Even in the early days,

00:18:03.856 --> 00:18:05.876
if you said, "A computer on
every desk in every home",

00:18:05.876 --> 00:18:07.070
and you'd say, "Okay, how many homes

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:07.935
"are there in the world?

00:18:07.935 --> 00:18:09.553
"How many desks are there in the world?

00:18:09.553 --> 00:18:11.453
"Can I make $20 for every home,

00:18:11.453 --> 00:18:13.009
"$20 for every desk?"

00:18:13.009 --> 00:18:15.243
You could get these big numbers,

00:18:15.243 --> 00:18:18.576
but part of the beauty
of the whole thing was

00:18:18.576 --> 00:18:21.624
we were very focused on the here and now.

00:18:21.624 --> 00:18:23.539
"Should we hire one more person?

00:18:23.539 --> 00:18:26.126
"If our customers didn't pay us,

00:18:26.126 --> 00:18:29.663
"would we have enough
cash to meet the payroll?"

00:18:29.663 --> 00:18:33.251
We really were very practical
about that next thing

00:18:33.251 --> 00:18:35.572
and so involved in

00:18:35.572 --> 00:18:38.503
the deep engineering that we
didn't get ahead of ourselves.

00:18:38.503 --> 00:18:41.063
We never thought how big we'd be.

00:18:41.063 --> 00:18:42.313
I remember when

00:18:43.741 --> 00:18:48.670
one of the early lists of
wealthy people came out.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:50.949
One of the Intel founders was there.

00:18:50.949 --> 00:18:54.006
The guy who ran Wang Computer Factory,

00:18:54.006 --> 00:18:55.980
Wang was still doing well.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:59.998
We thought, "Boy, if the
software business does well,

00:18:59.998 --> 00:19:02.602
"the value of Microsoft
could be similar to that",

00:19:02.602 --> 00:19:05.008
but it wasn't a real focus.

00:19:05.008 --> 00:19:09.175
The everyday activity of
just doing great software

00:19:10.186 --> 00:19:11.306
drew us in.

00:19:11.306 --> 00:19:12.670
Some decisions we made,

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:14.119
like the quality of the people,

00:19:14.119 --> 00:19:15.810
the way we were very global,

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:19.520
the vision of how we
thought about software,

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:22.299
that was very long-term.

00:19:22.299 --> 00:19:23.837
Other than those things

00:19:23.837 --> 00:19:27.053
we just came into work everyday,

00:19:27.053 --> 00:19:28.736
wrote more code,

00:19:28.736 --> 00:19:30.989
hired more people.

00:19:30.989 --> 00:19:35.172
It wasn't really until
the IBM PC succeeded

00:19:35.172 --> 00:19:38.106
and perhaps even until
Windows succeeded that

00:19:38.106 --> 00:19:39.546
there was a broad awareness

00:19:39.546 --> 00:19:42.046
that Microsoft was very unique

00:19:43.041 --> 00:19:44.054
as a software company

00:19:44.054 --> 00:19:45.641
and that these other companies had been

00:19:45.641 --> 00:19:47.114
one-product companies,

00:19:47.114 --> 00:19:49.019
hadn't hired people,

00:19:49.019 --> 00:19:50.707
couldn't do a broad set of things,

00:19:50.707 --> 00:19:52.340
didn't renew their excellence,

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:53.923
didn't do research.

00:19:55.269 --> 00:19:57.955
We thought we were doing
something very unique,

00:19:57.955 --> 00:20:01.705
but it was easily not
until 1995 or even 1997

00:20:03.595 --> 00:20:04.988
that there was this wide recognition

00:20:04.988 --> 00:20:09.155
that we were the company that
had revolutionized software.

